{
  "requirement": "Number of hexadecimal digits in the identity hash of an anonymous selector segment.\n\n```rust,ignore\npub const HASH_DIGITS: usize = 12;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject `requirements.sdk.outlines.HASH_DIGITS` states one obligation. The crate must publicly export `pub const HASH_DIGITS: usize = 12;`, the number of hex digits in an anonymous selector segment's identity hash. Its only {test} anchor is `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]`.\n\nThat test covers the obligation in three ways:\n- `specified_exports` reads the inventory in `md/requirements/sdk/public-api.md`. The inventory lists `const HASH_DIGITS` with a declaration link to this requirement. `documented_exports` walks the rustdoc HTML generated for the crate. It fails if the inventory and rustdoc differ in either direction, so the constant must exist as a public `const` at the documented path.\n- For each non-module export, `documented_exports` calls `check_declaration`. That function opens the linked requirement text in `md/requirements/sdk/outlines.md` and extracts the `rust,ignore` block. That block is the declaration `pub const HASH_DIGITS: usize = 12;`. It normalizes this block with `signature_parts` and compares it with the declaration rustdoc published for the item. Any difference, missing or unexpected, fails the test.\n- The comparison covers the whole declaration text, including the type `usize` and the literal value. I did not run rustdoc. I assume it prints a literal const's initializer in the item declaration, as it normally does. Given that, changing the value from 12, for example to 16, would make the published declaration differ from the requirement's block and fail the test.\n\nThe requirement states no further behavior. The use of the constant in parsing, in `parse_segment` and the outline builders, is covered by other requirements, and I did not assess it here. I found no unaddressed MUST, SHOULD, boundary or failure case for this subject. The test is marked with {test} references in the source and via the `:test:` anchor.",
  "references": "34070f30c190da855a0af0db8758db88cea74ad388475c8cd2f1dfaa1fb9eb00",
